      <button class="lang-toggle" id="langToggle" type="button">DE</button>
      <button class="theme-toggle" id="themeToggle" type="button" aria-label="Toggle light and dark theme" title="Toggle light / dark">
        <svg class="sun" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="4.2"/><path d="M12 2.5v2.2M12 19.3v2.2M4.2 12H2M22 12h-2.2M5.6 5.6l1.6 1.6M16.8 16.8l1.6 1.6M18.4 5.6l-1.6 1.6M7.2 16.8l-1.6 1.6"/></svg>
        <svg class="moon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20.5 14.8A8.2 8.2 0 0 1 9.2 3.5a8 8 0 1 0 11.3 11.3z"/></svg>
      </button>
    </span>
  </div>
</nav>

<header class="hero" id="top">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="hero-grid">
      <div>
        <span class="kicker" data-i18n="hero.kicker">The visual user manual</span>
        <h1 data-i18n="hero.h1">One product, scored across <span>five dimensions</span> of sustainability.</h1>
        <p class="lead" data-i18n="hero.lead">A visual companion to the <b>KPI&nbsp;List</b> workbook — a product-focused catalogue of sustainability indicators. Raw measurements at the bottom flow upward and combine into a single composite <b>Sustainability</b> score. Explore the live hierarchy below.</p>
        <div class="meta-pills" id="heroPills"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-art" aria-hidden="true">
        <!-- connected technical system: five domain nodes feeding one composite -->
        <svg viewBox="0 0 300 260" role="img">
          <g stroke="var(--art-line)" stroke-width="1.5">
            <line x1="150" y1="130" x2="150" y2="26"/>
            <line x1="150" y1="130" x2="270" y2="90"/>
            <line x1="150" y1="130" x2="238" y2="216"/>
            <line x1="150" y1="130" x2="62"  y2="216"/>
            <line x1="150" y1="130" x2="30"  y2="90"/>
          </g>
          <g>
            <circle cx="150" cy="26"  r="13" fill="var(--bg)" stroke="#35b567" stroke-width="3"/>
            <circle cx="270" cy="90"  r="13" fill="var(--bg)" stroke="#3f95e0" stroke-width="3"/>
            <circle cx="238" cy="216" r="13" fill="var(--bg)" stroke="#17c0cc" stroke-width="3"/>
            <circle cx="62"  cy="216" r="13" fill="var(--bg)" stroke="#f5911f" stroke-width="3"/>
            <circle cx="30"  cy="90"  r="13" fill="var(--bg)" stroke="#a875ff" stroke-width="3"/>
          </g>
          <circle cx="150" cy="130" r="30" fill="var(--green)"/>
          <text x="150" y="137" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff" font-family="Archivo,sans-serif" font-size="20" font-weight="800">Σ</text>
        </svg>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

<!-- BIG PICTURE -->
<section id="picture">
  <div class="wrap reveal">
    <span class="kicker" data-i18n="pic.kicker">The big picture</span>
    <h2 data-i18n="pic.h2">From five domains to one score</h2>
    <p class="lede" data-i18n="pic.lede">At the very top sits one number. It comes from five domains. Each domain breaks into sub-scores, and those into deeper scores, down to the raw data points someone must supply.</p>
    <div class="bigpic">
      <div class="crown">
        <div class="t" data-i18n="crown.t">Sustainability</div>
        <div class="s" data-i18n="crown.s">composite of the five domains</div>
      </div>
      <div class="connector"></div>
      <div class="fan" id="fan"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="flow-note" data-i18n="pic.flownote">
      The domains are <b>not fully separate</b>. A few raw inputs feed parents in more than one domain, so the structure is really a <b>network</b>, not five isolated trees. The same measurement can affect several aspects of sustainability.
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- EXPLORER -->
<section id="explorer">
  <div class="wrap reveal">
    <span class="kicker" data-i18n="exp.kicker">Interactive</span>
    <h2 data-i18n="exp.h2">Explore the hierarchy</h2>
    <p class="lede" data-i18n="exp.lede">This tree comes from the actual master catalogue. Pick a domain, expand the branches, and click any indicator to see what it measures, how the engine computes it, and the standards behind it. Grey leaves marked <span class="mini data" style="vertical-align:middle">DATA</span> are the raw numbers a company supplies.</p>
    <div class="explorer" style="margin-top:18px">
      <div class="ex-top">
        <div class="domtabs" id="domtabs" role="tablist"></div>
        <div class="ex-search">
          <input id="search" type="search" placeholder="Search indicators…" autocomplete="off">
          <button class="ex-btn" id="expandAll" data-i18n="exp.expand">Expand</button>
          <button class="ex-btn" id="collapseAll" data-i18n="exp.collapse">Collapse</button>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="ex-body">
        <div class="treepane" id="tree"></div>
        <div class="detail" id="detail">
          <div class="empty">
            <div style="font-size:2rem;color:var(--teal)">◆</div>
            <div>Select an indicator from the tree to see its full definition.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- LEVELS -->
<section id="levels">
  <div class="wrap reveal">
    <span class="kicker" data-i18n="lvl.kicker">Structure</span>
    <h2 data-i18n="lvl.h2">Five levels, colour-coded</h2>
    <p class="lede" data-i18n="lvl.lede">Every indicator sits at one of five levels. Higher-level scores are darker. The Level-5 raw data points are the lightest grey, and those are the ones you supply numbers for.</p>
    <div class="levels" id="levelLegend"></div>
    <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:20px">
      <div class="card">
        <h4 data-i18n="lvl.card1.h4">The ID scheme</h4>
        <p data-i18n="lvl.card1.p">Each domain has a letter prefix. <code>EN0</code> is the domain score. <code>EN1</code>, <code>EN2</code>… are Level-2 sub-scores. Add a digit per level down (<code>EN11</code>, <code>EN131</code>). Raw data points take <b>a hyphen</b> (<code>EN1-1</code>). The ID itself encodes the tree.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card" style="border-left:4px solid var(--warn)">
        <h4 data-i18n="lvl.card2.h4">⚠ A naming collision to watch for</h4>
        <p data-i18n="lvl.card2.p">In <b>Resource Efficiency</b> the IDs <code>R0</code>–<code>R9</code> look identical to the circular-economy <b>R-principles</b> <code>R0</code>–<code>R9</code> (R2 = <em>Reduce</em>) in the glossary. They are <b>completely different things</b>. Always read <code>R2</code> by context.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- AGGREGATION -->
<section id="aggregation">
  <div class="wrap reveal">
    <span class="kicker" data-i18n="agg.kicker">How it computes</span>
    <h2 data-i18n="agg.h2">Read top-down, calculate bottom-up</h2>
    <p class="lede" data-i18n="agg.lede">You read the tree top-down to understand it, but numbers flow <b>upward</b>. You supply the Level-5 raw data points. Each score combines its children and feeds its parent, up to the domain score and finally the composite.</p>
    <div class="card" style="margin-top:18px">
      <div class="lbl" data-i18n="agg.example" style="font-size:.71rem;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.09em;color:var(--muted);font-weight:700;margin-bottom:10px">A worked example: the carbon-footprint branch</div>
      <div id="ladder"></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- STRATEGIES -->
<section id="strategies">
  <div class="wrap reveal">
    <span class="kicker" data-i18n="str.kicker">The engine</span>
    <h2 id="stratHeading">Calculation strategies</h2>
    <p class="lede" data-i18n="str.lede">Every row's <em>Calculation Strategy</em> says how the row produces its value. Leaves are raw values. Above them a row is either a <b>score</b> (always 0–1) or a <b>raw total</b> (a sum or formula in kg, €, …). The number on each card is how many indicators use that strategy.</p>
    <div class="grid g3" id="stratGrid" style="margin-top:18px"></div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- WEIGHTS & NORMALIZATION -->
<section id="weights">
  <div class="wrap reveal">
    <span class="kicker" data-i18n="w.kicker">Per-company settings</span>
    <h2 data-i18n="w.h2">Weights &amp; normalization</h2>
    <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:14px">
      <div>
        <h3 data-i18n="w.h3a">Weights sum to 1</h3>
        <p data-i18n="w.pa">When a score combines several children, a <b>weight</b> says how much each child counts. Under any one parent, the weights of all direct children should add up to <b>1</b>, keeping every score on the same 0–1 scale. The template ships <b>equal 1/N defaults</b>, and each company overrides them. If a whole branch has no data, its weight effectively becomes 0, so the branch drops out instead of dragging the score down.</p>
        <div class="callout info" data-i18n="w.info" style="margin-top:6px"><b>1 = good, 0 = bad</b> everywhere. The page shows scores as a percentage of that 0–1 fraction.</div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 data-i18n="w.h3b">Normalization &amp; direction</h3>
        <p data-i18n="w.pb">Min-max normalization maps raw values to 0–1: <code>(Value − Min) / (Max − Min)</code>. The workbook has <b>no separate higher/lower switch</b>. Whichever bound is larger sets the direction.</p>
        <div class="normbox" style="margin-top:12px">
          <div class="band">
            <h4 data-i18n="w.up.h4">Higher is better</h4>
            <div style="font-size:.82rem;color:var(--muted)" data-i18n="w.up.sub">shares, ratings, efficiency</div>
            <div class="gradient up"></div>
            <div class="bandlbl" data-i18n="w.bandlbl"><span>Min&nbsp;=&nbsp;worst</span><span>Max&nbsp;=&nbsp;best</span></div>
            <div style="font-size:.8rem;margin-top:6px" data-i18n="w.up.note">Min &lt; Max (ascending band)</div>
          </div>
          <div class="band">
            <h4 data-i18n="w.down.h4">Lower is better</h4>
            <div style="font-size:.82rem;color:var(--muted)" data-i18n="w.down.sub">intensity, time, cost</div>
            <div class="gradient down"></div>
            <div class="bandlbl" data-i18n="w.bandlbl"><span>Min&nbsp;=&nbsp;worst</span><span>Max&nbsp;=&nbsp;best</span></div>
            <div style="font-size:.8rem;margin-top:6px" data-i18n="w.down.note"><b>Invert:</b> set Min &gt; Max</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="callout" data-i18n="w.warn" style="margin-top:12px"><b>Mind the direction.</b> For e.g. energy-per-unit where 25 kWh is worst and 0 is ideal, set <b>Min&nbsp;=&nbsp;25, Max&nbsp;=&nbsp;0</b>. A 5 kWh product then scores 0.8. An ordinary ascending band would score it backwards.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- WORKFLOW -->
<section id="workflow">
  <div class="wrap reveal">
    <span class="kicker" data-i18n="wf.kicker">For practitioners</span>
    <h2 data-i18n="wf.h2">The recommended workflow</h2>
    <p class="lede" data-i18n="wf.lede">The key sequence: <b>understand top-down → gather data first → set weights and min/max afterwards.</b></p>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li><b data-i18n="wf.s1b">Read the Overview &amp; Top-Level sheets.</b><span data-i18n="wf.s1s">Orient yourself: the goal, the structure, the R-principles, and what each of the five domains means.</span></li>
      <li><b data-i18n="wf.s2b">Pick a domain and read it top-down.</b><span data-i18n="wf.s2s">Read from the Level-1 score down to the Level-5 raw data points to see why each input matters and where it fits.</span></li>
      <li><b data-i18n="wf.s3b">Identify the raw inputs you must supply.</b><span data-i18n="wf.s3s">They are the grey <code>Data? = x</code> rows. The Metrics List sheet is the flat checklist of all of them, with links back to context.</span></li>
      <li><b data-i18n="wf.s4b">Gather the data first.</b><span data-i18n="wf.s4s">Use each row's Unit, Data Source (often LCA) and Life-Cycle Stages to know what to measure — before worrying about weights.</span></li>
      <li><b data-i18n="wf.s5b">Then set the per-company values.</b><span data-i18n="wf.s5s">Fill in Weights (sum to 1 under each parent) and the Target Min / Target Max reference values. Mind the direction for lower-is-better metrics.</span></li>
      <li><b data-i18n="wf.s6b">Consult the References as needed.</b><span data-i18n="wf.s6s">To check the basis of any KPI, follow its Reference code to the References sheet.</span></li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- GLOSSARY -->
<section id="glossary">
